# CoachDesk BD — environment variables
# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is gitignored and must stay that way.

# --- Supabase: Settings -> API in your project dashboard ---

# Safe to expose to the browser. RLS is what protects the data, not this key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-publishable-key

# SERVER ONLY. Bypasses every RLS policy. Never import this into a client
# component, never log it, never commit it. Used only by admin/cron routes.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=your-service-role-key

# --- SMS gateway (Month 2, leave blank until the sender ID is registered) ---
#
# The delivery worker refuses to run until all three of these are set, so a
# half-filled block is the same as an empty one — deliberately. A deployment with
# a key and no URL would report "gateway live" on the settings screen and then
# fail every message it was given.
SMS_API_URL=
SMS_API_KEY=
SMS_SENDER_ID=

# What the aggregator's response body looks like when it ACCEPTED the message.
#
# Set this the same day the account goes live, before the first real send. Every
# aggregator in this market answers HTTP 200 for business failures — `1002 Sender
# Id not correct`, `{"Status":"3"}`, sometimes an empty body — and a 200 taken at
# face value marks the message 'sent', refunds nothing, and tells the teacher the
# parent was told. Examples: `202` (bulksmsbd), `"statusCode":"200"` (MiM SMS),
# `"Status":"0"` (Elitbuzz). Left blank, a 2xx is trusted.
SMS_SUCCESS_RE=

# --- Signup's phone gate ---
#
# An owner proves the number before an account is made, and the code is sent by the
# gateway above — so the gate is on exactly when those three variables are filled. No
# variable turns it off once they are.
#
# This one turns it on early, for local work, with the code written to the server log
# instead of sent. It is ignored when NODE_ENV is production, which is every Vercel
# build including previews, so it cannot break a real signup by putting a code
# somewhere the visitor cannot read it. Leave it blank unless you are testing the flow.
OTP_DEV_TRANSPORT=

# --- The delivery worker: /api/cron/sms ---

# The only thing standing between the internet and a centre's credits. Any long
# random string; generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Callers send it as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>`. Vercel Cron adds that header
# by itself once this variable exists in the project. Unset, the endpoint refuses
# every request — it never falls open.
CRON_SECRET=

# Optional. Messages per run, and how many may be in flight at once. The defaults
# (10 and 5) finish a worst-case run in about 8 seconds, which fits inside Vercel
# Hobby's ~10s ceiling. Raise the plan before raising these; a killed run leaves
# its batch for the reaper and delays those messages by ten minutes.
SMS_BATCH_SIZE=
SMS_CONCURRENCY=

# --- Sales contact, shown on the plan screen ---
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC, so it reaches the browser: it ends up in a wa.me link and a tel:
# link, and the reader taps both. Nothing secret — it is a number you want
# customers to have.
#
# Left blank, the two contact buttons are not rendered at all and the plan screen
# still explains every tier. A WhatsApp button that opens a chat with nobody is
# worse than no button, on the one screen that exists to be trusted.
#
# Any Bangladeshi mobile format works (01712345678, +8801712345678,
# 8801712345678) — the page normalises it. A landline will not: WhatsApp needs a
# mobile, and the chip is built from the same number as the call.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SALES_PHONE=
